    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "strs = [\"eat\",\"tea\",\"tan\",\"ate\",\"nat\",\"bat\"]\n->  [[\"eat\",\"tea\",\"ate\"], [\"tan\",\"nat\"], [\"bat\"]]\n\nstrs = [\"\"]     ->  [[\"\"]]\nstrs = [\"a\"]    ->  [[\"a\"]]"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The idea: a key that every anagram shares"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Comparing every pair of words with Valid Anagram would be O(n²) comparisons. Instead, give each word a **canonical form** — a key that is the same for all its anagrams and different for everything else — and put words into a hash map under their key. Each group is then just one map entry."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Two keys work. The word's letters **sorted** (`\"eat\"`, `\"tea\"` and `\"ate\"` all sort to `\"aet\"`). Or its **letter counts** (one a, one e, one t: `\"a1e1t1\"`), which is the frequency-counting pattern again and avoids sorting."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Sorted letters as the key"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function groupAnagrams(strs) {\n  const groups = new Map();                   // sorted letters -> the words with them\n  for (const word of strs) {\n    const key = [...word].sort().join(\"\");    // anagrams sort to the same string\n    if (!groups.has(key)) groups.set(key, []);\n    groups.get(key).push(word);\n  }\n  return [...groups.values()];\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def group_anagrams(strs):\n    groups = {}                           # sorted letters -> the words with them\n    for word in strs:\n        key = \"\".join(sorted(word))       # anagrams sort to the same string\n        groups.setdefault(key, []).append(word)\n    return list(groups.values())"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "List<List<String>> groupAnagrams(String[] strs) {\n    Map<String, List<String>> groups = new HashMap<>();   // sorted letters -> the words with them\n    for (String word : strs) {\n        char[] letters = word.toCharArray();\n        Arrays.sort(letters);\n        String key = new String(letters);                 // anagrams sort to the same string\n        groups.computeIfAbsent(key, x -> new ArrayList<>()).add(word);\n    }\n    return new ArrayList<>(groups.values());\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "vector<vector<string>> groupAnagrams(vector<string>& strs) {\n    unordered_map<string, vector<string>> groups;        // sorted letters -> the words with them\n    for (const string& word : strs) {\n        string key = word;\n        sort(key.begin(), key.end());                    // anagrams sort to the same string\n        groups[key].push_back(word);\n    }\n    vector<vector<string>> result;\n    for (auto& entry : groups) result.push_back(entry.second);\n    return result;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n · k log k), space O(n · k)** for n words of up to k letters: each word is sorted, and the map stores every word plus a key."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Letter counts as the key"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Count each word's letters into 26 counters and turn the counts into a short string: each letter that appears, followed by how often. In Python a **tuple** of the 26 counts can be the key directly."
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "group-anagrams",
      "caption": "Each word's letter counts become its signature; words with the same signature land in the same group."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function groupAnagrams(strs) {\n  const groups = new Map();                   // signature -> the words that share it\n  for (const word of strs) {\n    const count = new Array(26).fill(0);\n    for (const ch of word) count[ch.charCodeAt(0) - 97]++;\n    let key = \"\";                             // e.g. \"eat\" -> \"a1e1t1\"\n    for (let k = 0; k < 26; k++) {\n      if (count[k] > 0) key += String.fromCharCode(97 + k) + count[k];\n    }\n    if (!groups.has(key)) groups.set(key, []);\n    groups.get(key).push(word);\n  }\n  return [...groups.values()];\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def group_anagrams(strs):\n    groups = {}                           # letter counts -> the words with them\n    for word in strs:\n        count = [0] * 26\n        for ch in word:\n            count[ord(ch) - ord(\"a\")] += 1\n        groups.setdefault(tuple(count), []).append(word)   # a tuple can be a dict key\n    return list(groups.values())"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "List<List<String>> groupAnagrams(String[] strs) {\n    Map<String, List<String>> groups = new HashMap<>();   // signature -> the words that share it\n    for (String word : strs) {\n        int[] count = new int[26];\n        for (int i = 0; i < word.length(); i++) count[word.charAt(i) - 'a']++;\n        StringBuilder key = new StringBuilder();          // e.g. \"eat\" -> \"a1e1t1\"\n        for (int k = 0; k < 26; k++) {\n            if (count[k] > 0) key.append((char) ('a' + k)).append(count[k]);\n        }\n        groups.computeIfAbsent(key.toString(), x -> new ArrayList<>()).add(word);\n    }\n    return new ArrayList<>(groups.values());\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "vector<vector<string>> groupAnagrams(vector<string>& strs) {\n    unordered_map<string, vector<string>> groups;        // signature -> the words that share it\n    for (const string& word : strs) {\n        int count[26] = {0};\n        for (char ch : word) count[ch - 'a']++;\n        string key;                                      // e.g. \"eat\" -> \"a1e1t1\"\n        for (int k = 0; k < 26; k++) {\n            if (count[k] > 0) key += char('a' + k) + to_string(count[k]);\n        }\n        groups[key].push_back(word);\n    }\n    vector<vector<string>> result;\n    for (auto& entry : groups) result.push_back(entry.second);\n    return result;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n · k), space O(n · k).** Counting a word is linear in its length, and building the key reads 26 counters — a constant."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "complexity",
      "rows": [
        {
          "approach": "Sorted letters as the key",
          "time": "O(n · k log k)",
          "timeWhy": "Each of the **n** words is **sorted** to make its key.",
          "space": "O(n · k)",
          "spaceWhy": "The map stores **every word** plus its key."
        },
        {
          "approach": "Letter counts as the key",
          "time": "O(n · k)",
          "timeWhy": "Counting a word is **linear**; the key reads 26 counters.",
          "space": "O(n · k)",
          "spaceWhy": "The map stores **every word** plus its key."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "For short words the difference is small, and the sorted key is the one most people write first. Mention the counting key when the interviewer asks whether you can avoid the sort."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Edge cases to test"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Empty strings** — `[\"\", \"\"]` is one group of two empty strings.",
        "**Same letters, different counts** — `\"aab\"` and `\"abb\"` must land in different groups; a key built from *which* letters appear (without counts) gets this wrong.",
        "**No anagrams at all** — every word is a group of one.",
        "**Joining counts with no separator** — counts `[1, 11, …]` and `[11, 1, …]` both become `\"111…\"`. Keep a letter or a comma with every count, as here."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Variations"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Group Shifted Strings** — `\"abc\"` and `\"xyz\"` belong together: the key is the list of gaps between neighbouring letters.",
        "**Find All Anagrams in a String** — the same counts, compared inside a sliding window. Pattern 5.",
        "**Valid Anagram** — one pair instead of a whole list. First lesson of this pattern."
      ]
    }
  ],
